      "content": "Here's a clinical case study for a patient presenting to the ER with a suspected broken femur:\n\n**Patient Information:** Mr. Russell, a 30-year-old male, presents to the ER after attempting to muscle-pull a heavy object up the stairs. He complains of severe, stabbing pain in his left thigh that radiates down to his knee. Upon arrival, Mr. Russell is diaphoretic (sweating) and upset, with a heart rate of 120 bpm and a blood pressure of 180/100 mmHg. He is able to answer questions like a rational patient but exhibits behavioral changes, including a look of severe distress and refusal to move his left leg.\n\nWhen assesssed, Mr. Russell says the pain is sudden and severe, radiating from the \"bottom of his leg to the knee\", which he describes as feeling like \"a hot knife stabbing\". He winces with movement, especially when his left leg is bent at the knee. When the nurse applies gentle pressure at the level of the hip, Mr. Russell offers no audible reaction, but his eyes tense up.",
